  "body": {
    "id": "thm:sharp-degree-frontier",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "For every fixed \\(B\\ge1\\), every fixed \\(0<c\\le1\\), and every integer sequence \\(1\\le d_n\\le n\\), \\[K_n(d_n;B,c)\\longrightarrow0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad d_n=o(n^{2/3}).\\] No additional slowly varying boundary factor is present.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "B",
      "c",
      "n",
      "d_n",
      "K_n(d;B,c)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:degree-envelope",
      "thm:uniform-normal-bound",
      "thm:repeated-star-boundary",
      "def:repeated-star-witness"
    ],
    "justification": "This candidate iff statement is the field-tier target, contingent on D0 verification of both the martingale upper bound and the repeated-star limit.",
    "gap": "No cited paper gives a necessary-and-sufficient degree condition for the fixed \\(r=2\\) CGD estimator; \\cite{KandirosPipisDaskalakisHarshaw2024CGD} gives only \\(1/9\\) sufficiency and a nonsharp obstruction.",
    "consumer": "A verified frontier would supply a single asymptotic design rule for deciding when CGD normal inference is uniformly justified.",
    "proof_tex": "Suppose first that \\(d_n=o(n^{2/3})\\).  By \\(\\mathrm{thm:uniform\\text{-}normal\\text{-}bound}\\),\n\\[\n K_n(d_n;B,c)\\le C_{\\mathrm N}(B,c)\n \\left(\\frac{d_n^{3/2}}n\\right)^{1/5}\\longrightarrow0.\n\\]\n\nConversely, suppose \\(d_n\\) is not \\(o(n^{2/3})\\).  There are \\(\\epsilon>0\\) and a subsequence, still denoted by \\(n\\), on which \\(d_n\\ge\\epsilon n^{2/3}\\).  Fix \\(0<\\beta<\\epsilon\\), and use \\(\\mathrm{def:repeated\\text{-}star\\text{-}witness}\\) with \\(k_n=\\lfloor\\beta n^{2/3}\\rfloor\\).  Then \\(k_n+1\\le d_n\\) eventually on this subsequence, and \\(\\mathrm{thm:repeated\\text{-}star\\text{-}boundary}\\) supplies a member of \\(\\mathcal M_n(d_n;B,c)\\) whose standardized estimator converges weakly to \\(\\mathcal L_\\beta\\).\n\nLet \\(F_\\beta\\) be the distribution function of \\(\\mathcal L_\\beta\\).  It is continuous because the law has a Gaussian component.  Pointwise convergence of distribution functions to a continuous distribution function is uniform: choose finite tail cutoffs and a finite grid on which the increments of \\(F_\\beta\\) are small, and use monotonicity between adjacent grid points.  Therefore, along the chosen subsequence,\n\\[\n d_{\\mathrm K}\\left(\\frac{T_n-\\tau_n}{\\sigma_n},Z_0\\right)\n \\longrightarrow \\sup_z|F_\\beta(z)-\\Phi(z)|=:\\delta_\\beta.\n\\]\nThe last quantity is strictly positive because \\(\\mathrm{thm:repeated\\text{-}star\\text{-}boundary}\\) proves \\(\\mathcal L_\\beta\\ne\\mathcal N(0,1)\\).  Since \\(K_n(d_n;B,c)\\) is a supremum over a class containing this witness, it cannot converge to zero.  This proves the equivalence.  The same critical power appears on both sides with arbitrary fixed \\(\\beta>0\\), so no extra slowly varying boundary factor is present.",
